# Verification inventory

These checks cover small properties, not the correctness of the whole package.
The Python example is instructional and does not run in the production package.
A verified copy of an algorithm does not verify the production implementation.

| Function | Property | Tool | Assumptions/bounds | Status |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `verification/python/index_contract.py:normalize_index` | Result is in bounds and equals `index % length` | CrossHair 0.0.113 | Positive length, valid input index, 10-second search per condition | Example |
| `normalize_index` (same instructional example) | Same properties on generated concrete examples | Hypothesis 6.170.15 | Positive lengths and valid indices | Example |
{% if use_rust %}
| Production Rust `count_primes` and `sum_primes` | Exact counts and sums match reference values | Kani 0.68.0 | Every `u64` limit in `0..=8`; unwind 10 with unwinding assertions enabled | Example |
{% endif %}

After a check runs, record its date, tool version, source revision, result, assumptions, and bounds.
Use honest statuses: `Example`, `Checked in CI`, `Failed`, `Not checked`, or `Proof established`.
Do not mark the examples as checked merely because the template includes them.
A proof claim must name the exact property, input domain, and tool assumptions.

## Python checks

The development group pins Hypothesis. The optional `verification` group pins CrossHair and locks its solver dependencies.
CrossHair runs only on Python 3.11. Its group restriction does not change the normal Python support matrix.

Run concrete tests:

```sh
uv run --frozen pytest
```

{% if use_rust %}
Build the extension with `uv run --frozen maturin develop` before the full Python suite.
{% endif %}
Run the standalone example without a package build:

```sh
uv run --frozen --python 3.11 --only-group dev --only-group verification pytest -o addopts= tests/test_index_contract.py
uv run --frozen --python 3.11 --only-group dev --only-group verification crosshair check --report_all --per_condition_timeout 10 verification/python/
```

The contract command is also available as `uv run --frozen poe verify-python`.
The separate `formal-verification` workflow uses the same commands on one Linux runner.
The full Python/OS matrix does not run CrossHair.

For useful package assurance, attach contracts to selected production functions that the package actually calls.
Replace the example target with those modules. Use `--group verification` instead of the two `--only-group` flags when the target needs the installed package.
Small pure helpers for indexing, offsets, identifiers, validation, and boundary conditions are good candidates.
Use Hypothesis and integration tests for unsupported NumPy/Pandas operations, native extension internals, databases, networks, and complex mutable state.

Hypothesis checks generated concrete examples. These tests are not mathematical proofs.
CrossHair searches for counterexamples through symbolic execution. A successful exit does not prove that every input satisfies the contract.
Inspect its `--report_all` results. Timeouts and unknown conditions remain gaps, not verified properties.

{% if use_rust %}
## Rust checks

The core crate contains a Kani harness next to the production functions that the Python extension calls.
It does not verify a separate implementation. Read its input assumptions and unwind annotation before interpreting a result.
The small bounded domain is an instructional starting point, not a claim about arbitrary production input sizes.

Install the pinned driver and its matching compiler:

```sh
cargo install --locked kani-verifier --version 0.68.0
cargo kani setup
cargo kani -p {{ python_package_name }}_core
```

Kani setup installs its own compatible Rust toolchain. CI bootstraps with Rust 1.99.1.
After setup, `uv run --frozen poe verify-rust` runs the same harness.
The separate Linux Kani job does not change `cargo test` or require Kani for normal development.
Its meaningful bounded harness runs on pull requests. Branch protection remains a maintainer decision.
If you remove the harness, make the job manual until meaningful production properties exist again.

Kani checks assertions and safety properties under the stated assumptions and bounds.
An insufficient unwind bound, excluded real input, unsupported operation, or failed check is not a successful verification.
Kani fits indexing, integer arithmetic, parsing state, conversions, and bounds checks.

{% endif %}
